   "id": "git-vs-github",
   "topic": "Basics",
   "lv": "e",
   "q": "What is the difference between Git and GitHub?",
   "hint": "A tool against a service.",
   "answer": "Git is a distributed version control tool that runs on your computer and stores history in a .git folder. GitHub (like GitLab and Bitbucket) is a hosting service for Git repositories that adds pull requests, code review, issues, permissions and CI/CD such as GitHub Actions.",
   "explain": "<p>You can use Git with no server at all. The hosting service is where the team meets and where automation runs.</p>",
   "points": [
    "Git is the tool",
    "GitHub hosts repositories",
    "Collaboration and CI on top"
   ]
