   "topic": "Basics",
   "lv": "e",
   "q": "Explain the working tree, the staging area and the repository.",
   "hint": "Where does a change live after edit, add and commit?",
   "answer": "The working tree holds the files you edit. git add copies a change into the staging area (the index), which is the draft of the next commit. git commit stores the staged snapshot permanently in the repository as a commit object.",
   "explain": "<p>The staging area lets you commit part of your work, for example with git add -p, so each commit stays focused on one change.</p>",
   "points": [
    "Edit in the working tree",
    "git add stages",
    "git commit records the snapshot"
   ]
