   "topic": "Basics",
   "lv": "m",
   "q": "Does Git store differences or snapshots?",
   "hint": "Think of a commit and its tree.",
   "answer": "Each commit points to a tree that describes the complete project at that moment, so logically Git stores snapshots. Unchanged files reuse the same blob, so nothing is duplicated. Inside packfiles Git also stores similar objects as deltas, but that is a storage detail.",
   "explain": "<p>This is why switching branches and comparing any two commits is fast: Git reads two trees, it does not replay a chain of patches.</p>",
