   "topic": "Branching and merging",
   "lv": "m",
   "q": "When does git merge make a fast forward, and when a merge commit?",
   "hint": "Has the target branch moved since you branched?",
   "answer": "If the current branch has no commits that the other branch lacks, Git just moves the pointer forward: a fast forward, no new commit. If both sides have new commits, Git does a three way merge using the merge base and creates a merge commit with two parents. --no-ff forces a merge commit, --ff-only refuses anything but a fast forward.",
   "explain": "<p>Teams that want each feature visible as a unit in history use --no-ff. Teams that want a straight line rebase first and then fast forward.</p>",
